
  That split is the crux: a settings module has to live in one of those worlds, and the browser one currently has no
  module system at all.

  [1mThe fork:[0m how does the app know which environment it's in? Everything else follows from that, because a static page
  has no [38;5;153mprocess.env[39m to read.

  - [1mHostname detection[0m — settings module maps [38;5;153mlocation.hostname[39m to a base URL. Zero build tooling, no manual edit when
    deploying, works today. Costs: the full env table ships to the client, and it only distinguishes environments that
    have distinct hostnames.
  - [1mHand-edited constant[0m — one [38;5;153mconst ENV = "dev"[39m at the top of the module. Simplest possible thing, obvious to read.
    Costs: changing environments is a code edit and a commit, which is the friction you're asking me to remove.
  - [1mBuild-time injection[0m — add a bundler/env-var step that substitutes the endpoint. Most conventional for real
    deployments. Costs: introduces a toolchain, a build step, and dependencies to a repo that has none; also the
    heaviest to undo.

  Cheap to change later: the hostname map's contents, adding environments. Expensive to change later: adopting a build
  step (it reshapes [38;5;153mindex.html[39m, the module format, and the dev loop), and the module format itself, since it determines
  whether [38;5;153msrc/[39m and the browser can ever share the settings module.

  [1mMy recommendation: hostname detection.[0m It matches the repo's no-build character, needs no edit to switch environments,
  and stays compatible with adding a build step later if this grows.
